+## core-0008: current corpse-decay clamp
+
+Patch:
+
+- `patches/core-0008-current-corpse-decay-clamp.patch`
+
+Files to watch:
+
+- `src/game/Objects/Creature.h`
+
+Purpose:
+
+- expose the configured corpse delay read-only;
+- expose one generic operation that can only shorten the currently running
+  corpse-decay timer.
+
+Why this remains a core patch:
+
+Penqle exposes `SetCorpseDelay()`, `SetRespawnTime()` and `RemoveCorpse()`, but the
+currently running `m_corpseDecayTimer` is private. Changing the configured delay
+after death does not update the already-running corpse timer. Reconstructing that
+private state in the module is not possible without either approximate timing or a
+broader forced-despawn path.
+
+The Playerbot already calls `AccelerateRespawn()` once when XP is awarded and again
+after `CMSG_LOOT_RELEASE`. Therefore no persistent per-corpse limit is needed: after
+Penqle performs its native post-loot timer recalculation, the second call can clamp
+the resulting current timer again. This keeps `AllLootRemovedFromCorpse()` entirely
+core-native.
+
+Upstream retirement condition:
+
+- Penqle exposes an equivalent public getter/current-corpse timer reduction API.
+
+Rebase-sensitive areas:
+
+- `m_corpseDelay` and `m_corpseDecayTimer` units;
+- death-state initialization of the current corpse timer.
+
+Regression checks:
+
+- neither helper can lengthen the current corpse lifetime;
+- normal Creature death/loot behavior is byte-for-byte core-native outside callers
+  that explicitly request the clamp;
+- Playerbot acceleration retains the historical 20-second lower bound while loot
+  remains;
+- the post-loot Playerbot call clamps Penqle's freshly recalculated timer without
+  storing state across corpse lifecycles.
+
 ## Update workflow
 
 Before modifying the working branch, record the Penqle base commit. After applying
